    "ef8aef9197fa09669f7d": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions by nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned many dynasties, with significant reinforcement and expansion occurring during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which is when much of the well-preserved stone and brickwork seen today was built.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed from stone and brick.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall at intervals, used for observation, defense, and housing troops.\n*   **Stairways/Ramps:** Steep, constructed staircases are visible, providing access to the elevated sections of the wall and towers.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top edges of the walls, designed to provide cover for defenders.\n*   **Material:** The construction utilizes rammed earth, stone, and fired brick, characteristic of later Ming Dynasty construction.",
