    "7865b85e6a511d6e653c": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical function was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and well-preserved sections visible today often date back to the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which undertook massive reconstruction and reinforcement projects.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall line, used for observation, defense, and housing soldiers.\n*   **Stairways/Access Paths:** Steep, fortified staircases are visible, providing access to the different levels of the wall and towers.\n*   **Ramparts:** The top walkway of the wall, designed for troop movement and defense.\n*   **Stone/Brick Construction:** The primary building material visible is large, durable stone and brickwork.",
    "0326de8c09ee4d921369": {"image": "ChineseWall", "prompt": "Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "7865b85e6a511d6e653c"}, "digest": "25a6550c30b56c858c4f27c88414e29da0dcc25417205316fa3b3d496cc0ddcb", "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: extensive feature inventory repeats visible details and is longer than requested.", "answer_sha256": "92f4c2650cb132925896d69bf433fa4ced4ff19e209abf738700a9a750a6d0f8", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}, "finish_reason": "stop"},
