    "85dd1e0631f00e65d3a1": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions, particularly by nomadic groups from the northern steppes. The most famous and extensive sections visible today were largely constructed and reinforced during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Walls:** The primary defensive structure, built with stone and brick, forming a continuous barrier along the ridgelines.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, multi-story structures (like the one on the left) built into the wall to serve as observation posts, barracks, and defensive strongholds.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, stone-built staircases are visible, providing access up the steep, mountainous terrain to the wall's various levels.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top sections of the wall feature defensive parapets, though the specific crenellations are not clearly detailed from this distance.\n*   **Integration with Landscape:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural topography of the rugged, hilly terrain.",
    "8f08e37dd7a7aff144e2": {"label": "ChineseWall · 93.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Visual detail -2: says crenellations unclear though visible. Clarity -5: substantially overlong.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-hip-n1-mtp0-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "85dd1e0631f00e65d3a1"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
