    "3d83cc2648a7003e8e2c": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built and rebuilt by various dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various Eurasian peoples. The most famous and well-preserved sections, like the one shown, date largely from the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed of stone and brick.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Large, square or rectangular structures built into the wall, serving as defensive strongholds, observation posts, and barracks.\n*   **Stairways/Ramps:** Steep, constructed staircases leading up the steep terrain to access the wall and towers.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The defensive wall structure along the top of the wall, designed for soldiers to defend from.\n*   **Masonry:** The construction utilizes heavy, durable materials, primarily brick and stone, characteristic of later imperial construction.",
    "815323a70eecba7004a1": {"label": "ChineseWall · 93.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Correctness -2: dates pictured section to Ming era. Clarity -5: overlong.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-hip-n2-mtp1"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "3d83cc2648a7003e8e2c"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
