    "4b089165d2feafd695f1": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical function was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions by nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The Ming Dynasty (1368–1644) undertook the most extensive and recognizable construction using brick and stone, which is what is often seen today.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall system (like the larger towers visible on the right) used for observation, defense, and housing troops.\n*   **Stairways/Stairs:** Steep, stone-built staircases leading up and down the defensive structures, allowing for rapid movement of soldiers.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The defensive walls along the top of the curtain walls, providing cover for defenders.\n*   **Material:** The construction utilizes rammed earth, stone, and fired brick, characteristic of later Ming Dynasty construction.",
    "b74eca9338748c6552e2": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: lengthy feature list exceeds the concise request.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-hip-n2-mtp1-q4_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "4b089165d2feafd695f1"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
