    "3c043e34138ebaa673a2": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions, particularly from nomadic groups of the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned numerous dynasties, with significant building and reinforcement occurring during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which is when much of the well-preserved stone and brickwork seen today was constructed.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** Long, imposing defensive walls constructed primarily of stone and brick, forming the main defensive barrier.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall at intervals. These served as garrisons, observation posts, and signaling stations.\n*   **Stairways/Ascending Paths:** Steep, constructed staircases are visible, allowing defenders and troops to move quickly along the wall and up to the towers.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top edge of the wall features defensive parapets, providing cover for soldiers.\n*   **Integration with Terrain:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous landscape.",
    "2da3e61a6498f9b2d39a": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: extensive multi-part answer exceeds concise request.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-hip-n2-mtp1-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "3c043e34138ebaa673a2"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
