    "c17a678733293dd8ffa8": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical function was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and extensive sections seen today were largely constructed during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls built along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortified Towers:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall at regular intervals, used for observation, signaling, and defense.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, stone-built staircases leading up to the wall, allowing defenders and travelers access to the ramparts.\n*   **Stone Masonry:** The construction utilizes durable stone and brickwork, characteristic of later Ming Dynasty fortifications.\n*   **Natural Integration:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous terrain for maximum defensive advantage.",
