    "5a41de017337310fa3a7": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions, particularly from nomadic groups of the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned numerous dynasties, with significant building and reinforcement occurring during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which is when much of the well-preserved stone and brickwork seen today originates.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls made of stone and brick.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall, used for defense, observation, and housing troops.\n*   **Staircases/Access Points:** Steep, fortified staircases are visible, providing access to the higher sections of the wall and towers.\n*   **Ramparts:** The top walkway of the wall, designed for soldiers to patrol and defend.\n*   **Material:** The construction utilizes traditional materials, primarily brick and stone, integrated into the rugged, mountainous terrain.",
    "e5197aad3ab97183ca54": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: lengthy feature list exceeds the concise request.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-hip-n4-mtp0-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "5a41de017337310fa3a7"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
