    "6bfff410fdab464ac3b6": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and extensive sections visible today were largely constructed during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls built along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall at intervals, serving as defensive strongholds, observation posts, and barracks.\n*   **Stairways/Ascending Paths:** Steep, constructed stairways are visible, allowing defenders and travelers to ascend and traverse the wall.\n*   **Stone/Brick Construction:** The primary building material visible is large, weathered stone and brickwork.\n*   **Integration with Terrain:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous landscape for maximum defensive advantage.",
