    "a38304e04a535d9f7cec": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and well-preserved sections, like the one shown, were largely constructed or significantly reinforced during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The main defensive structure, built with stone and brick, forming a continuous barrier along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, multi-story structures (like the one on the left) that served as defensive strongholds, barracks, and observation posts.\n*   **Stairways/Access Paths:** Steep, stone-built staircases are visible, providing access to the wall and towers for defenders.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top edges of the wall feature defensive parapets, allowing soldiers to observe and fire upon attackers.\n*   **Strategic Placement:** The wall is visibly integrated into the rugged, mountainous terrain, utilizing the natural topography for defense.",
    "51012b92f1549249484b": {"label": "ChineseWall · 93.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Correctness −3: “like the one shown” guesses that the pictured section was built or reinforced in the Ming period. Clarity −4: the long history and five expanded feature sections are not concise; the visible parapets, towers, stairs, masonry, and terrain are otherwise covered.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-vulkan-n1-mtp1"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "a38304e04a535d9f7cec"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
