    "4b25e494d547d2b4f7e2": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical function was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions, particularly from nomadic groups of the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned numerous dynasties, with significant building and reinforcement occurring during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which is when much of the well-preserved stone and brickwork seen today was constructed.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls made of stone and brick that form the main barrier.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures (like the large one on the left) built into the wall, which served as barracks, observation posts, and defensive strongholds.\n*   **Stairways/Ascents:** Steep, fortified staircases are visible, allowing defenders to move quickly up and down the wall and to the towers.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top sections of the walls feature defensive parapets for soldiers.\n*   **Integration with Terrain:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous landscape for maximum defensive advantage.",
    "29647025ea51779fb5a8": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: lengthy feature list exceeds concise request.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-vulkan-n2-mtp0-q4_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "4b25e494d547d2b4f7e2"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
