    "d0d16cb7c1b4a7c6b646": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical function was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and well-preserved sections visible today often date back to the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which undertook massive reconstruction and reinforcement projects.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed of stone and brick.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built along the wall, serving as defensive strongholds, observation posts, and barracks.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, constructed staircases leading up to the wall and towers, allowing for troop movement and surveillance.\n*   **Battlements/Parapets:** The top sections of the wall, designed to provide cover for defenders.\n*   **Stone/Brick Construction:** The primary building material used for the fortifications.",
    "1d5666dbb4e4ef26a866": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: lengthy answer exceeds concise request.", "Configurations: gemma-e4b-Q4_0-vulkan-n4-mtp0-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "d0d16cb7c1b4a7c6b646"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
