    "94f21ed951e1d156b21d": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built, rebuilt, and maintained by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions by nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and well-preserved sections visible today often date back to the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls constructed of stone and brick.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built along the wall, serving as defensive strongholds, observation posts, and barracks.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, constructed staircases leading up to the wall and towers, allowing for rapid movement of troops and surveillance.\n*   **Material:** The construction utilizes large amounts of stone and fired brick, characteristic of later, more durable Ming-era construction.",
