    "1e708bd4a74a858529d6": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the historical northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several dynasties, beginning as early earthworks in the 7th century BC. The most well-preserved sections, like the one pictured, were primarily built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)**. Its primary purposes were to protect the Chinese empire against invasions from nomadic groups to the north, regulate trade along the Silk Road, and control immigration and emigration.\n\n### Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing weapons, and signaling nearby units using smoke or fire.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall provided cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous stretches of wall connecting the towers, constructed from stone, brick, and rammed earth.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated paths that follow the natural topography of the mountain ridges, allowing for the rapid movement of troops.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of local stone, kiln-fired bricks, and lime mortar, which provided the durability seen in the Ming-era construction.",
    "51e704077e64adeda469": {"label": "ChineseWall · 93.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Correctness -2: says the pictured section was Ming-built. Clarity -5: lengthy feature list.", "Configurations: gemma12-Q4_0-hip-n4-mtp0-q4_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "1e708bd4a74a858529d6"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
