    "c23aea2a178c4652ecc3": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks during the Spring and Autumn period (770–476 BCE). The most famous sections were built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)**, which utilized brick and stone to create the durable structures seen today. Its primary purpose was to protect the Chinese empire against invasions from nomadic groups to the north, while also serving as a means of border control and the regulation of trade along the Silk Road.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and signaling nearby units.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall, designed to provide cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Steep, integrated stone steps and pathways that allow for the movement of troops and horses across rugged terrain.\n*   **Curtain Walls:** Long, continuous stretches of masonry connecting the towers, built to follow the natural contours of the mountain ridges.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of kiln-fired bricks, stone blocks, and rammed earth, often bonded with a traditional mortar made of lime and sticky rice.",
    "76ed9d77b0c9f0f72b40": {"label": "ChineseWall · 95.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Clarity -5: lengthy feature list exceeds the concise request.", "Configurations: gemma12-Q4_0-hip-n4-mtp1"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "c23aea2a178c4652ecc3"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
