    "7d25bf40fe4d3eec7404": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n**History:**\nConstruction began as early as the 7th century BC, with various dynasties (most notably the Ming Dynasty, which built much of the stone and brick structure seen today) expanding and connecting different walls. Its primary purpose was to protect the Chinese empire from invasions by nomadic groups from the north, while also serving as a means of border control, trade regulation, and communication.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and spotting approaching enemies.\n*   **Crenellated Parapets:** The notched tops of the walls provided cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Battlements:** The raised walkways along the top of the wall allowed for the rapid movement of troops.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated stone steps and sloping paths that navigate the steep, rugged mountain terrain.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of rammed earth, stone, and kiln-fired bricks, often featuring a distinctive reddish-brown hue.",
    "175cf6a679982c55faf0": {"label": "ChineseWall · 93.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Correctness -2: states trade regulation and communication as definite purposes without qualification. Clarity -5: lengthy list.", "Configurations: gemma12-Q4_0-vulkan-n1-mtp0-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "7d25bf40fe4d3eec7404"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
