    "ae937113875817ef6ecd": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the historical northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks in the 7th century BC. The most well-preserved sections, like the one pictured, were primarily built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)**. Its primary purposes were to protect the Chinese empire against invasions from nomadic groups to the north, regulate trade along the Silk Road, and serve as a border control system.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and signaling nearby units via smoke or fire.\n*   **Crenellated Parapets:** The notched tops of the walls provided cover for archers and soldiers while allowing them to fire upon enemies.\n*   **Stepped Stairways:** Integrated paths that allow for the movement of troops and supplies across the steep, rugged mountainous terrain.\n*   **Curtain Walls:** Long stretches of defensive walls connecting the towers, constructed using a combination of brick, stone, and rammed earth.\n*   **Gateways:** Strategic openings (visible in the foreground) that controlled access to specific sections of the wall.",
    "2356391320e024a09982": {"label": "ChineseWall · 89.00%", "paragraphs": ["Identify this place and explain its history. Mention visible architectural features. Be concise and do not guess the exact section.", "Finish: stop", "Correctness -5: asserts unidentifiable foreground gateways and assigns the photographed stretch to Ming construction despite the no-section-guess instruction. Completeness -1: adds speculative Silk Road purpose. Clarity -5: far longer than requested. Gateway claim charged once under correctness.", "Configurations: gemma12-Q4_0-vulkan-n1-mtp1-q8_0"], "text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "ae937113875817ef6ecd"}, "image": "ChineseWall", "src": "../media/qengineering/ChineseWall.jpg"},
    "91920a5f313087781ef7": "This is a skyline of **Singapore**.\n\nThe visible landmarks and their positions are as follows:\n\n*   **Marina Bay Sands:** Located on the far left of the image, it is easily identifiable by its three distinct towers topped with a large, boat-shaped \"Sands SkyPark.\"\n*   **Singapore Flyer:** This is the large observation wheel located on the right side of the image.\n*   **Marina Bay Sands and Central Business District (CBD) Skyline:** The cluster of skyscrapers between the Marina Bay Sands and the Singapore Flyer makes up the city's financial district.\n*   **ArtScience Museum:** This is the lotus-shaped building visible in the center-left area, nestled among the skyscrapers near the waterfront.",
