    "d8edf2262c3d481c4f17": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n**History:**\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks in the 7th century BC. The most well-preserved sections, like the one pictured, were built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)** to protect the empire against invasions from northern nomadic groups. It served as a military defense system, a border control point, and a transportation route for troops and trade.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and signaling nearby units.\n*   **Crenellations:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall, designed to provide cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Battlements:** The raised wall sections that allow defenders to look out over the landscape.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated paths that allow for the rapid movement of troops and horses along the uneven mountain terrain.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of kiln-fired bricks, stone blocks, and rammed earth, which helped the structure withstand harsh weather and heavy use.",
    "42cf7ff61f9d248eaddc": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "d8edf2262c3d481c4f17"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 27, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: lengthy descriptions of materials, troop movement, and construction go beyond the requested concise answer. Visual detail: also presents rammed earth as material of this pictured section, which cannot be established from its visible masonry.", "answer_sha256": "9b51faaeee313abd4daf9253379ffa4b425d3fbb01de54cb59be04fe1b1de8b2", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
    "6b7e3ea30f452a6269c2": "This is a skyline of **Singapore**.\n\nThe visible landmarks and their positions are:\n\n*   **Marina Bay Sands:** Located on the far left of the image, it is easily identifiable by its three towers topped by a large, boat-shaped \"SkyPark.\"\n*   **Singapore Flyer:** Located on the right side of the image, it is a large observation wheel.\n*   **Marina Bay Sands / Central Business District (CBD) Skyline:** The cluster of skyscrapers in the center-left represents the city's financial district.\n*   **ArtScience Museum:** Located in the center, near the base of the skyscrapers, it is recognizable by its lotus-like architectural shape.",
