    "5330ee0d2a12848c6dae": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the historical northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthwork barriers in the 7th century BC. The most well-preserved sections visible today, like the one pictured, were primarily built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)**. It was designed to protect the Chinese empire against invasions from nomadic groups to the north, serve as a border control point, and facilitate trade and communication along the Silk Road.\n\n### Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick towers used for housing soldiers, storing weapons, and providing a vantage point for surveillance.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall allowed archers and soldiers to fire while remaining protected.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated stone steps and pathways that follow the natural topography of the mountain ridges.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of kiln-fired bricks, stone blocks, and rammed earth, often bonded with a traditional mortar made of lime and sticky rice.\n*   **Curved Walls:** The structure follows the \"dragon\" style, winding along the crests of hills to maximize defensive visibility.",
    "3bbfb534d792db1500e7": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "5330ee0d2a12848c6dae"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 27, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: lengthy details about materials, trade, mortar, and terrain crowd out the concise visual description. Visual detail: also presents rammed earth as material of this pictured section, which cannot be established from its visible masonry.", "answer_sha256": "0d679193a73c07b620b081784337e81cc42c00cad7047c59e066e7cd7a24c249", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
