    "5963e72c390d68477c25": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a massive fortification system built across the historical northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks during the Spring and Autumn period (770–476 BCE). The most famous sections were built during the **Qin Dynasty** (221–206 BCE) to connect existing walls and protect against nomadic invasions. The majority of the well-preserved stone and brick structures seen today were built or reconstructed during the **Ming Dynasty** (1368–1644) to defend against Mongol and Manchu tribes.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick towers used for housing soldiers, storing weapons, and signaling nearby units.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall, designed to provide cover for archers and defenders.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Steep, integrated stone steps and pathways that allow for the movement of troops and supplies across rugged terrain.\n*   **Curtain Walls:** Long, continuous stretches of masonry connecting the towers, built to follow the natural contours of the mountain ridges.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of local stone, kiln-fired bricks, and rammed earth, often reinforced with lime mortar.",
    "68e0a616290b75d157c5": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "5963e72c390d68477c25"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 27, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: extensive dynasty dates, building materials, and feature descriptions exceed the requested concise answer. Visual detail: also presents rammed earth as material of this pictured section, which cannot be established from its visible masonry.", "answer_sha256": "29e7cc65e4f7888c71016a60bb804b059a77c18f803e0f917dd65ff12f6770ad", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
