    "2fad4bc01ac9a95f84f5": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a historic fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early defensive walls in the 7th century BC. The most well-known sections were built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)** to protect the empire against invasions from northern nomadic groups. It served as a military defense system, a border control point, and a transportation corridor for trade and communication.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Large, multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and housing weapons.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched top edges of the walls, designed to provide cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Crenellations:** The specific tooth-like projections along the top of the wall.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Steep stone steps and inclined paths built to navigate the rugged, mountainous terrain.\n*   **Materials:** A combination of brick, stone, and rammed earth, with traditional tiled roofs on the watchtowers.",
    "489e4e9b4252f1f3efa2": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "2fad4bc01ac9a95f84f5"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 27, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: long descriptions of defense, signaling, and terrain repeat the core visible features. Visual detail: also presents rammed earth as material of this pictured section, which cannot be established from its visible masonry.", "answer_sha256": "daabe2a9b193cc3ca6143cda1d2ea73141b88c9c165d2fb014f69bf2dd8c5222", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
