    "fb6620f0ef2fd7ed317c": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a historic fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n**History:**\nConstruction began as early as the 7th century BC, with various dynasties contributing to its expansion and renovation. The most famous sections were constructed during the **Ming Dynasty** (1368–1644) to protect the empire against invasions from northern nomadic groups. It served as a military defense system, a border control point, and a trade route.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for surveillance and housing soldiers.\n*   **Crenellations:** The notched tops of the walls (merlons and embrasures) designed to provide cover for archers and defenders.\n*   **Battlements:** The defensive parapets running along the top of the wall.\n*   **Stairways:** Steep stone steps integrated into the wall to navigate the rugged, mountainous terrain.\n*   **Masonry:** A combination of stone, brick, and rammed earth construction, often featuring arched openings for structural support and visibility.",
    "32c3779f75096e2761f9": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "fb6620f0ef2fd7ed317c"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 27, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: long lists of masonry, roof, stair, and parapet details repeat the visible description. Visual detail: also presents rammed earth as material of this pictured section, which cannot be established from its visible masonry.", "answer_sha256": "42a838c897c1cf11c1c247a20d57f18bb4419bc6ecdd261c6b1e0c90a7130755", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
