    "fd8c22bc3edd34a513f9": "This is the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nConstructed over centuries, the wall was primarily built to protect the Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from various nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. While many sections were built in the Qin Dynasty (221–206 BC), much of the well-preserved stone and brick architecture visible today dates from the **Ming Dynasty** (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story defensive structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and housing weapons.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall designed to provide cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Stairways and Ramparts:** Wide walkways and steep stairs built to allow for the rapid movement of troops across rugged terrain.\n*   **Masonry Construction:** The use of stone, brick, and mortar to reinforce the wall against both physical assault and erosion.",
    "ac2c459f48404b472f94": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "fd8c22bc3edd34a513f9"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 29, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "clarity: the detailed construction and use descriptions exceed the requested concise account. Visual detail: mortar is not identifiable in the image; the visible wall is masonry.", "answer_sha256": "cceb3b7798e1d90ca487f4b0be930c3e378e08f6d903571055babd9406e8c75a", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
    "ab0f1c4e349e64b6423a": "This is a skyline of **Singapore**, specifically the Marina Bay area.\n\nThe visible landmarks and their positions are:\n\n*   **Marina Bay Sands:** Located on the far left of the image, it is the large, iconic three-tower complex topped with a continuous \"skypark\" structure.\n*   **Singapore Flyer:** Located on the right side of the image, it is the large observation wheel.\n*   **Marina Bay Financial Centre and surrounding skyscrapers:** These form the dense cluster of high-rise buildings in the center of the image, situated between the Marina Bay Sands and the Singapore Flyer.\n*   **ArtScience Museum:** This is the lotus-shaped building visible at the base of the skyline, near the center-left.",
