    "49bd98cac2f57bbb8d1a": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups of various peoples from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and extensive sections visible today were largely constructed or reinforced during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls built along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall line, used for observation, defense, and housing troops.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, stone-built staircases providing access up and down the defensive structures.\n*   **Stone/Brick Construction:** The primary building material visible in the walls and towers.\n*   **Strategic Placement:** The wall is clearly built along a natural ridge or elevated terrain, maximizing its defensive advantage.",
