    "52d4417977c871f4a534": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built, rebuilt, and maintained by various dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary purpose was to protect the Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions by nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned numerous dynasties, with the most famous and well-preserved sections often associated with the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls built along the ridge.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures (like the larger buildings visible) that served as defensive strongholds, barracks, and observation posts.\n*   **Stairways/Stairs:** Steep, stone-built staircases leading up the slopes to access the wall and towers.\n*   **Stone/Brick Construction:** The primary building material visible for the walls and towers.\n*   **Terraced/Hilly Setting:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous terrain.",
    "341f279d3438e1f6ee67": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "52d4417977c871f4a534"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: long explanations of the wall’s terrain integration and construction repeat details beyond the brief request.", "answer_sha256": "0f9239bacba12d51ba4b6faf7a031142e09241231ccd5df28ec0661f92f13fca", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
