    "2c3bbfc765647cb8df61": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast network of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over many centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary historical purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions, particularly from nomadic groups of the Eurasian Steppe. The construction spanned over 2,000 years, with the most famous and well-preserved sections often dating back to the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644).\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Curtain Walls:** The long, continuous defensive walls built along the ridge of the terrain.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, square or rectangular structures built into the wall (like the larger structures visible) used for observation, defense, and housing troops.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, stone-built staircases are visible, providing access to the different levels of the wall and fortifications.\n*   **Material:** The construction primarily utilizes stone and brick, characteristic of later Ming Dynasty construction, though older sections used rammed earth.\n*   **Integration with Landscape:** The wall is strategically built to follow and utilize the natural contours of the mountainous terrain for maximum defensive advantage.",
    "f048cc106d25e78b5e6b": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "2c3bbfc765647cb8df61"}, "review": {"correctness": 38, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "correctness: implies the pictured section belongs to the Ming period without evidence for that section; clarity: long construction and terrain explanations exceed the concise request.", "answer_sha256": "054691da4cdcca52e936be89095bb552b9d1003b8460b0ae55ec928bddbee5b0", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
