    "1937d5dc82e5be5a871f": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is a series of fortifications built across the historical northern borders of ancient Chinese states and Imperial China. Its construction spanned many dynasties, beginning as separate walls built by various states to protect against nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and extensive sections were built and reinforced during the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644) to defend against the Mongols. It represents centuries of military engineering and defense strategy.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Walls:** The primary feature is the massive, continuous defensive wall constructed from brick and stone.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Several rectangular, fortified towers are visible along the wall, serving as defensive strongpoints, garrisons, and observation posts.\n*   **Stairways/Access Points:** Steep, stone-paved staircases are visible, providing access to the higher sections of the wall and towers.\n*   **Parapets:** The top of the wall features crenellations (battlements), characteristic of defensive fortifications.\n*   **Integration with Terrain:** The wall is strategically built along the crest of a steep, rugged hillside, utilizing the natural topography for defense.",
    "8945c11926c05981b514": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "1937d5dc82e5be5a871f"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: extensive details about garrisons, terrain, and material construction repeat the main description.", "answer_sha256": "8239802f600e9ac99c0c6841a10e7f3b7a1cfe9eb3ee48eb061399c05da8a8b4", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
