    "10d4052301e2c011983b": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a vast system of fortifications built across the historical northern borders of ancient Chinese states and Imperial China. Its construction spanned many dynasties, beginning as early as the 7th century BCE, with major building and reinforcement occurring during the Qin, Han, and Ming dynasties. Its primary purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions by various nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Ramparts/Walls:** The primary feature is the massive, defensive wall constructed from stone and brick, featuring a consistent height and thickness.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Several fortified structures (towers) are visible along the wall, which served as defensive positions, barracks, and signal stations.\n*   **Stairways:** Steep, stone staircases are visible, providing access to different levels of the wall and towers, indicating the defensive and logistical nature of the structure.\n*   **Terrain Integration:** The wall is visibly integrated into the rugged, hilly, and mountainous landscape, demonstrating its strategic placement.",
    "633cbf411c89aed7190b": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "10d4052301e2c011983b"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 28, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "visual_detail: describes the wall, towers, and stairs but does not mention the visible crenellated parapets; clarity: extended historical and terrain discussion.", "answer_sha256": "63ed0e0aae777a74d63c16543fd3357041b95c5876c42884756a7d85070592d1", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
