    "525cc1336d821bfde47e": "This image depicts a section of the **Great Wall of China**.\n\n**History:**\nThe Great Wall is not a single continuous structure but a system of fortifications built and rebuilt by various Chinese dynasties over centuries, beginning as early as the 7th century BC. Its primary purpose was to protect Chinese states and empires against raids and invasions from nomadic groups from the Eurasian Steppe. The most famous and well-preserved sections visible today largely date from the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), which undertook massive reconstruction and fortification efforts.\n\n**Architectural Features:**\nThe visible features include:\n*   **Ramparts/Walls:** Extensive, high defensive walls constructed primarily of brick and stone.\n*   **Watchtowers/Fortifications:** Prominent, multi-story structures built along the wall, serving as defensive positions, barracks, and observation points.\n*   **Stairways:** Steep, stone-paved staircases providing access to the higher sections of the wall and towers.\n*   **Terrain Integration:** The wall is strategically built along and conforms to the rugged, hilly, and mountainous landscape.",
    "bae5ba73933bddd072ae": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "525cc1336d821bfde47e"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "clarity: extended historical and feature-by-feature explanation is longer than the requested concise description.", "answer_sha256": "f59d2b2d99d129802266d3a16d88f9e7b72ccf342f330d17478a2a3c37c79d7e", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
