    "a0685c60b0334199922c": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a series of fortifications built across the historical northern borders of China.\n\n### History\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks in the 7th century BC. The most famous sections, like the one pictured, were primarily built and reinforced during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)** to protect the empire against invasions from northern nomadic groups. It served as a military defense system, a border control mechanism, and a means of regulating trade and migration along the Silk Road.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing supplies, and providing a vantage point for surveillance.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched parapets along the top of the wall, designed to provide cover for archers and soldiers.\n*   **Crenellations:** The alternating raised and lowered sections of the wall's top edge.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated stone steps and sloping paths that allow for the rapid movement of troops and equipment along the rugged terrain.\n*   **Materials:** The use of local stone, kiln-fired bricks, and lime mortar, which have allowed the structure to endure for centuries.",
    "f99e4f5cdbc856fef8cc": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "a0685c60b0334199922c"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 29, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: adds an extended list of materials and defensive functions beyond the concise image description. Visual detail: mortar is not identifiable in the image; the visible wall is masonry.", "answer_sha256": "249ade4459558fa8115ae983f2b873666e5903e283e95c16cca1380d2ad22cbe", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
