    "9f2846c05f228c71acd6": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a historic fortification system built across the northern borders of China.\n\n**History:**\nThe wall was constructed over several centuries, beginning as early earthworks in the 7th century BC. The most well-preserved sections, like the one pictured, were primarily built during the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)** to protect the empire against invasions from northern nomadic groups. It served as a military defense system, a border control point, and a means of regulating trade and migration along the Silk Road.\n\n**Visible Architectural Features:**\n*   **Watchtowers:** Multi-story stone and brick structures used for housing soldiers, storing weapons, and providing a vantage point for surveillance.\n*   **Battlements:** The notched tops of the walls (crenellations) allowed archers and soldiers to fire while remaining protected.\n*   **Curtain Walls:** Long stretches of defensive walls connecting the towers, constructed from brick and stone.\n*   **Stairways and Ramps:** Integrated stone steps and pathways designed to facilitate the rapid movement of troops and supplies across the rugged mountainous terrain.\n*   **Arched Openings:** Visible in the watchtowers, these provided structural support and windows for observation.",
    "0096eb42fb4aa65b88bc": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "9f2846c05f228c71acd6"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "clarity: adds trade-route and troop-use detail and a long feature inventory beyond the concise description requested.", "answer_sha256": "d1d57bb8bf4967acf27073bde1d38944fe8825ccc000e4c1c15f3198c06f41d6", "reviewer": "gpt-6-luna"}},
