    "afc83ea0ba746f50935f": "This is the Great Wall of China, a series of fortifications built across northern China to protect against invasions. Visible features include watchtowers with tiled roofs, crenellated parapets, and steep stone staircases winding along mountain ridges. The wall’s construction spans centuries, with sections dating back to the Warring States period and major expansions under the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644). Its strategic placement on rugged terrain reflects its military purpose, while its enduring structure showcases advanced engineering and labor-intensive craftsmanship.",
    "e1833047af778101da0d": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "afc83ea0ba746f50935f"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "clarity: lengthy architectural and craftsmanship commentary goes beyond the requested concise history and visible features.", "answer_sha256": "f48baf8a8a7dbaafbc29e7ae99878ee304d18b966eddbe4f278b4bef02980f58"}},
    "8593946d0a06cef70e83": "This is **Singapore**, captured at dusk or dawn with the skyline reflected in the water of Marina Bay.\n\nVisible landmarks and their positions:\n\n- **Marina Bay Sands (left)**: The iconic three-tower hotel complex topped by a massive sky park, standing prominently on the left side of the frame.\n- **ArtScience Museum (center-left)**: The lotus-shaped building nestled between Marina Bay Sands and the central business district.\n- **Singapore Flyer (right)**: The giant observation wheel, towering over the waterfront on the right side of the image.\n- **City skyline (center)**: A dense cluster of modern skyscrapers, including the UOB Plaza and other financial district buildings, stretching across the middle ground.\n- **Waterfront promenade and gardens (foreground)**: Lush greenery and walkways line the bay, with reflections of the city lights shimmering on the calm water.\n\nThe composition beautifully balances architectural grandeur with natural serenity, showcasing Singapore as a vibrant, futuristic metropolis.",
    "b940b2d3d723307e608b": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "8593946d0a06cef70e83"}, "review": {"correctness": 39, "visual_detail": 28, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 7, "note": "correctness: adds promenade gardens and UOB Plaza as visible details that are not clearly established in this frame; visual detail: the foreground is open water, without clearly visible lush gardens or walkways; clarity: adds extended composition and city commentary beyond the brief landmark positions.", "answer_sha256": "7f3a03d02bd6d4c8a4c9fbf2c6431b57aa92f6fc9941c0e029debd39d7261ca9"}},
    "1119a9fe88c2e407fefe": "This scene is from the 1997 film **Titanic**, directed by James Cameron.\n\nIn this iconic moment, Jack Dawson (Leonardo DiCaprio) and Rose DeWitt Bukater (Kate Winslet) stand on the bow of the RMS Titanic as it sails through calm waters at sunset. With arms outstretched, they embrace the wind and the vast ocean horizon, embodying a sense of freedom, romance, and fleeting joy — a poignant contrast to the impending disaster that will soon unfold. The golden light of dusk bathes them in warmth, capturing one of cinema’s most memorable expressions of love and liberation.",
    "98d4be799508f90a4fb5": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "1119a9fe88c2e407fefe"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "clarity: adds extended romance, impending-disaster, and cinema-history commentary beyond the brief visible-scene description.", "answer_sha256": "5056501c371b6f98832aafa69ed1985336579744ec8b48474830ae389b05a111"}},
    "2312eadfee523b4862fb": "Based on the visible ingredients in the image, here are the components needed to make this pizza:\n\n**Dough Ingredients:**\n- Flour (likely “00” flour for Neapolitan-style pizza)\n- Water\n- Yeast (active dry or instant)\n- Salt\n- Possibly a small amount of olive oil or sugar (optional, not visible but common)\n\n**Toppings:**\n- San Marzano tomato sauce (or similar high-quality tomato puree)\n- Fresh mozzarella cheese (likely fior di latte or mozzarella di bufala, melted and slightly browned)\n- Fresh basil leaves (added after baking, as they appear bright green and unwilted)\n\nNote: The charred spots on the crust suggest it was baked in a very hot oven (e.g., wood-fired or deck oven), which is typical for authentic Neapolitan pizza — but no additional toppings beyond those listed are visible.",
    "0a8ae747b0d627e148a7": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "2312eadfee523b4862fb"}, "review": {"correctness": 40, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 8, "note": "clarity: adds optional flour details and oven speculation beyond the requested separated ingredient list.", "answer_sha256": "f8651e9ad68f5af9046982533f6f68ed9574982ea60a1cd1396ccce7bac7cf08"}},
    "497bd148df3a84b284ff": "This is the **Great Wall of China**, a series of fortifications built across northern China over centuries to protect against invasions.\n\n### Visible Architectural Features:\n- **Watchtowers**: Square or rectangular towers with crenellated tops, used for surveillance and defense.\n- **Battlements**: Notched parapets along the wall’s top for archers and soldiers.\n- **Stone and brick construction**: The wall is built from local materials — here, reddish-brown bricks and gray stone — showing regional variation.\n- **Steep staircases**: Wide, winding steps allow access along the ridge; some sections show erosion or repair.\n- **Merlons and embrasures**: Defensive openings in the walls for firing arrows while remaining protected.\n\n### History:\nConstruction began as early as the 7th century BC by various Chinese states, but major unification occurred under the **Ming Dynasty (1368–1644)**, which rebuilt and extended the wall using stone and brick. It stretches over 21,000 km and served as a military barrier, trade route (Silk Road), and symbol of imperial power. Today, it stands as a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the Seven Wonders of the Modern World.\n\n*(Note: Exact location not specified — could be near Mutianyu, Badaling, or another preserved section.)*",
    "925fcb4ed9a4d705d472": {"text": {"$ref": "image_answers", "id": "497bd148df3a84b284ff"}, "review": {"correctness": 34, "visual_detail": 30, "completeness": 20, "clarity": 6, "note": "correctness: says Ming unification occurred, and speculates that the pictured section might be Mutianyu or Badaling despite the instruction not to guess the section; clarity: extensive lengths, dynastic dates, trade, heritage, and feature details overwhelm the concise request.", "answer_sha256": "4175ae2cbeedfa831feeab7a99b1ad89c85c6043ddb591b012c0f21cbf308beb"}},
